  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study uses a difference-in-means refusal representation and activation interventions, but its abstention is elicited with a safety-constrained instruction on benign visual questions. It does not test the claim’s harmful-request refusal behavior arising without that inducement.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports abstention rates under default versus safety-constrained prompts, intervention outcomes, and figure-based analyses of visual influence and representation trajectories. The prompt deliberately encourages abstention, so these results do not test whether the claimed mechanism mediates naturally arising harmful-request refusals. Results cited from other work are excluded.",
    "test_reason": "The study borrows the difference-in-means steering recipe, but its direction is abstained-minus-answered, not harmful-minus-harmless. Its behaviour is abstention on benign VQA, induced by an instruction to say 'I cannot determine the answer'. That is a related but different construct (uncertainty abstention), deliberately prompted, so no claim version is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also conclude that abstained generations retain visual influence, so perception is preserved; that abstention-direction separability is architecture-dependent (strong in Qwen3-VL, weak in Phi-3-V); and that suppressing the direction, especially in late layers, restores grounded answers.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The direction is computed from abstained versus answered samples under a safety-constrained setup, rather than harmful versus harmless instructions. The study manipulates hidden activations at inference time in VLMs; it does not perform the original harmful-request refusal evaluation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Intervention effects vary by architecture, dataset, layer, and intervention strength. The text reports stronger effects in later layers and progressively stronger abstention with positive alpha.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A cautious system instruction raises abstention on benign VQA: Qwen3-VL from 0 to 0.28–0.56, Phi-3-V from ~0.05 to 0.36–0.44, LLaVA only from 0 to 0.03–0.13.",
        "proposed_claim": "Instruction-induced abstention in aligned VLMs on answerable visual questions varies strongly by model.",
        "row_ids": [
          "t1-qwen-vqa",
          "t1-qwen-gqa",
          "t1-qwen-pope",
          "t1-llava-vqa",
          "t1-llava-gqa",
          "t1-llava-pope",
          "t1-phi-vqa",
          "t1-phi-gqa",
          "t1-phi-pope"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Subtracting or adding an abstained-minus-answered mean-difference vector lowers or raises prompted abstention. Effects are large for Qwen3-VL, small for LLaVA, and moderate for Phi-3-V. Suppression on Qwen POPE raises incorrect answers from 0.03 to 0.33.",
        "proposed_claim": "A diff-in-means abstention vector steers prompted VQA abstention in VLMs, with model-dependent strength and accuracy costs.",
        "row_ids": [
          "t2-qwen-vqa-suppress",
